# Copy to .env and adjust. Never commit .env — this repository is public.
#
# Connection strings are deliberately absent. The application composes
# DATABASE_URL and REDIS_URL at runtime from the parts below, so each
# credential appears exactly once and no credential-shaped string is ever
# committed. See packages/config/src/env.ts.
#
# The values below are safe defaults for the local Docker stack only. They
# authenticate against a container on your own machine and are worthless
# anywhere else.

# --- Runtime -----------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=debug

# Collect and expose metrics on /metrics (slice H1). Defaults to true when
# absent — a service nobody can see the state of is what this ended. The
# endpoint is not reachable from the internet: only `web` joins the edge
# network, and Prometheus scrapes this from inside the stack.
METRICS_ENABLED=true

# --- API ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0.0.0.0 rather than localhost: inside a container, binding the loopback
# interface makes the service unreachable from outside it.
API_HOST=0.0.0.0
# 3001, not 3000: the Next dev server takes 3000 and they cannot share it.
API_PORT=3001

# --- Database ----------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_HOST=localhost
# 5433 rather than the default 5432, so this stack coexists with any other
# Postgres already running on the machine. Change freely — nothing hardcodes it.
POSTGRES_PORT=5433
POSTGRES_USER=rental
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=local_dev_only
POSTGRES_DB=rental_dev
# Separate database for the integration suite, so tests can truncate freely
# without destroying development data (BRD §12.4).
POSTGRES_TEST_DB=rental_test

# How the database connection is protected (ADR 0038). Left unset here because
# the local Postgres container speaks no TLS, and unset composes a URL with no
# sslmode at all — which is what it has always done.
#
# **Required when NODE_ENV=production**, where unset would mean a plaintext
# connection nobody chose. Three values, and only three:
#
#   disable      no TLS. Correct for the local container and a private network
#   no-verify    encrypted, certificate not checked
#   verify-full  encrypted, chain and hostname checked. Use this for Neon
#
# `require` is deliberately refused, and so are `prefer`, `allow` and
# `verify-ca`. pg 8 reads `require` as an alias for verify-full; pg 9 reads it
# as encrypted-but-unverified — so writing it lets a routine dependency bump
# downgrade database TLS with nothing failing. The three above mean the same
# thing in both versions. A misspelling is refused too: the driver treats an
# unrecognised value as "TLS on" and says nothing.
#
# POSTGRES_SSLMODE=

# --- Cache and queue ---------------------------------------------------------
REDIS_HOST=localhost
REDIS_PORT=6379

# --- Identity (ADR 0015) -----------------------------------------------------
# The API verifies Clerk session tokens and does nothing else with Clerk. It is
# deliberately NOT given CLERK_SECRET_KEY: that key can mint sessions, read the
# whole user directory and impersonate anyone, and none of that is needed to
# check a signature. The web app holds it; see apps/web/.env.local.example.
#
# Clerk's JWT signing key, PEM, on one line with \n escaped. A *public* key —
# there is nothing to protect here, and supplying it is what makes verification
# networkless, so a Clerk outage cannot hang an authenticated request.
#
# Derive it from the instance's published JWKS:
#
#   clerk api jwks | node -e "const {createPublicKey}=require('node:crypto');\
#   let s='';process.stdin.on('data',d=>s+=d).on('end',()=>{const k=JSON.parse(s).keys[0];\
#   console.log(JSON.stringify(createPublicKey({key:k,format:'jwk'})\
#   .export({type:'spki',format:'pem'}).toString()))})"
CLERK_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY=

# Origins whose tokens this API accepts, as the `azp` claim, comma-separated.
# Without it a token minted by any Clerk application on the instance verifies
# against our key set. This is the check that makes the audience mean anything.
# Local development runs the web app on 3000 and the API on 3001.
CLERK_AUTHORIZED_PARTIES=http://localhost:3000

# --- The admin second-factor escape hatch (ADR 0030) -------------------------
# Open every admin route to an administrator with no verified second factor.
#
# ADR 0021 requires a second factor for administrative access and reads Clerk's
# `fva` claim to prove it. Clerk gates every MFA strategy — and passkeys —
# behind a paid plan, so on the free plan there is no second factor anybody can
# enrol, and the guard correctly refuses every administrator everywhere. That
# left four slices whose main surface no human had ever used, including a
# statutory confirmation.
#
# Set it to exactly `true` to open the admin pages locally. Anything else,
# including the literal string `false`, leaves the check on.
#
# **The API refuses to start if this is set with NODE_ENV=production.** Not
# ignored, not warned about — refused, because a flag quietly dropped in
# production is one somebody eventually believes is working. Never set it in
# `infra/compose`, in a secret manager, or anywhere a deployed process reads.
DANGEROUSLY_ALLOW_ADMIN_WITHOUT_MFA=false

# --- Personal data encryption (ADR 0016) -------------------------------------
# Encrypts the identifying part of a stored address — the street lines. The
# postcode and town stay in clear because Phase 3 has to geocode the first and
# publish the second, and a value the search index needs cannot be ciphertext.
#
# A base64 32-byte key:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Three things about it that are easy to get wrong:
#
#   - It is NOT recoverable and there is no plaintext copy. Restoring the
#     addresses table from a backup without this key restores unreadable rows,
#     so the key belongs in the backup plan and must be stored separately from
#     the database it protects.
#   - Staging and production must use different keys. A shared one means a
#     staging dump decrypts production addresses.
#   - The API holds it. The worker deliberately does not — a queue consumer has
#     no business being able to read a home address.
PERSONAL_DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Internal triggers -------------------------------------------------------
# The shared secret the worker presents to set off scheduled work, and the API
# checks (slice 4.7a, ADR 0048). Both processes read it; they must agree.
#
# Generate one with:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Required, and both processes refuse to start without it. That is deliberate:
# an optional secret has to be handled at the guard, and the tempting handling
# is "none configured, so skip the check" — which is an unauthenticated
# mutating endpoint reached by forgetting a line in this file.
#
# Notes:
#   - It is not personal data and not a provider credential, but it IS a
#     credential. Secret manager, never an image, and different per environment.
#   - Rotating it means restarting the API and the worker. There is deliberately
#     no support for two valid secrets at once: the pair is deployed together,
#     and a rotation window is a second code path nobody would exercise.
#   - It authenticates a machine and carries no identity. Nothing downstream may
#     infer a user, a role or a permission from it.
INTERNAL_TRIGGER_SECRET=

# Where the worker reaches the API to set off scheduled work (slice 4.7b).
# **Worker only** — the API never reads it, which is why it lives in its own schema
# (`loadWorkerEnv`) rather than the shared one.
#
# 3001 locally, because the API runs there: the Next dev server takes 3000. Deployed
# it is `http://api:3000`, set by `infra/compose/docker-compose.app.yml` rather than
# by an env file, because it is a compose service name rather than a choice.
#
# Required, with no default. A default would be wrong in one of the two environments
# and wrong *silently*: the worker would start, the schedule would fire, and every
# sweep would fail against an address nobody chose.
API_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:3001

# The port the worker serves /metrics on (slice H6). Worker only.
#
# Defaulted, unlike API_INTERNAL_URL above, and the difference is the point: a
# wrong address makes every sweep fail silently, so that one has to be typed. A
# wrong metrics port makes a scraper miss a target — loud in Prometheus as
# `up == 0`, harmless to the work, and identical in every environment we run.
#
# 9464 is the conventional Prometheus exporter port. Nothing publishes it: the
# worker joins the internal network only.
# WORKER_METRICS_PORT=9464
